select m.id, 3, $L$Mapping the Current SDLC$L$, $L$19 minute read$L$, $L$You cannot assess where quality is created or lost until you can see the whole flow of a change from idea to production. This lesson teaches you to map the software delivery lifecycle as it truly operates — including the queues, hand-offs and rework that no process diagram admits to — and to overlay QA's touchpoints onto it. The map you build here is the substrate for the next two lessons: bottlenecks and capability gaps only make sense against a picture of the whole system.$L$, array[$L$Map the real SDLC of an organisation as a value stream, capturing hand-offs, queues and rework rather than the idealised process$L$, $L$Overlay QA touchpoints, feedback loops and quality gates onto the flow to see where quality is built in versus inspected in$L$, $L$Distinguish process (touch) time from lead time and use the gap to locate where the system actually loses time and quality$L$], $L$## Map reality, not the wiki
Every organisation has a documented process. Almost none works the way the document claims. Your job is to map the **actual** path a change takes — the one you reconstruct by walking a real, recently shipped change with the people who did the work, not the one on the Confluence page. Ask "show me the last thing that went to production and walk me through everything that happened to it."

## Value-stream thinking for QA leaders
Borrow the core distinction from value-stream mapping:
- **Process time (touch time)**: time someone is actively working on the change.
- **Lead time**: total elapsed time from request to done, including all the waiting.
- **Flow efficiency**: process time divided by lead time. In most software delivery it is shockingly low — the change spends most of its life waiting in queues, not being worked on.

For a QA leader this is powerful because the places where quality is lost are usually the **hand-offs and queues**, not the touch-time activities. A defect injected in a vague requirement sits latent through every downstream queue until testing finally exposes it — expensively.

## What to capture at each stage
For every stage from idea to production, capture:
- **Who does it and what triggers it** (the entry condition).
- **The output and its quality** (is it fit to hand on?).
- **The wait before it** (the queue) and the wait after it.
- **Rework loops** — where does work bounce backwards, and how often?
- **The feedback delay** — how long between injecting a defect and discovering it?

## Overlay the QA touchpoints
Now draw where QA actually engages:
- **Requirements / refinement**: is QA present, or does it first see the work at code-complete? At Northstar, QA is pulled in late — so the map will show a long stretch of the value stream with no quality feedback at all.
- **During development**: are there unit and API checks running per commit (quality built in), or does the first real test happen after hand-off (quality inspected in)?
- **The CI/CD gates**: what actually runs on every PR, and what gates a release? Northstar runs build + unit tests on PR, then a semi-automated deploy a human approves — so the map must show where automated confidence ends and manual judgement begins.
- **The regression stage**: Northstar's ~5-day manual regression is a single enormous stage near the end. On the map it dominates the lead time and sits after most of the value has been built — the definition of quality inspected in, late.
- **Post-release feedback**: how do production defects flow back? Rising production defects with weak escaped-defect metrics means the feedback loop from production to the team is slow and lossy.

## Built-in vs inspected-in quality
The single most valuable thing the map reveals is *when* quality feedback happens relative to *when* defects are injected. A healthy system builds quality in continuously (short feedback loops close to the work). An immature one inspects quality in at the end (one long, late, manual gate). Northstar's map will visibly show the second pattern: a long, feedback-free front half and a heavy, manual, late-stage inspection.

## Watch the environment and data as first-class stages
Test environments and test data are not background details — for many teams they are where lead time goes to die. If Northstar's shared staging is unstable and full of "someone else's data", then "waiting for a usable environment" and "constructing test data" are real stages that belong on the map with their own queue times. Assessors who leave them off systematically under-count the delay.

## How you would explain this to senior stakeholders
Show the map with lead time and process time side by side. "A change takes 23 elapsed days but only about 4 days of actual work — the other 19 are queues, hand-offs and a 5-day manual regression at the very end. We are not slow because people are slow; we are slow because quality feedback happens late and in big batches." That sentence reframes QA from a bottleneck into the function that can compress the elapsed time — which is exactly the story that gets you investment.$L$, $L$You map one real Northstar change end to end — a payments-related story Marcus's squad shipped. Walking it with the squad, the actual flow emerges: idea and a thin ticket (a Figma link and a sentence) → 3 days waiting for a developer → 2 days development with unit tests on PR → code-complete → *now* QA first sees it → 1 day waiting for a stable staging slot → half a day rebuilding test data someone else had overwritten → exploratory and manual checks find two defects rooted in the ambiguous requirement → back to development → re-test → the change then waits to be bundled into the next major release → ~5-day manual regression pass → human-approved deploy.

Lay process time against lead time and the picture is stark: perhaps 4–5 days of touch time inside roughly three elapsed weeks. The two defects were injected at the very first stage — the vague requirement — and not discovered until QA engaged after code-complete, so they travelled through every queue in between before anyone caught them. The 5-day regression and the environment/data waits dominate the elapsed time, and both sit at the end.

The map makes three things undeniable without you having to assert them: quality is inspected in late rather than built in early; the requirements stage is where defects are injected; and environments and test data are genuine time sinks, not footnotes. You have not yet named a single bottleneck or gap — but you have built the picture that makes the next two lessons obvious.$L$, $L$- Mapping the documented process instead of walking a real change — the map then flatters the organisation and hides the queues
- Recording only the working stages and omitting the waits, so the map under-counts lead time and misses where quality is actually lost
- Leaving test environments and test-data provisioning off the map when they are among the biggest sources of delay
- Drawing QA as a single late box rather than showing every point where quality feedback does — or fails to — happen
- Presenting a map with no time on it; without lead time versus process time, leadership sees a diagram, not a diagnosis$L$, $L$Map with the people who did the work, in the room, walking one specific change — never alone from documentation. The moment a developer says "well, actually it usually sits here for a few days waiting for a free environment" you have found a queue no process document would ever have told you about. The map's value is in the waits people are slightly embarrassed to admit to.$L$, $L$Take the last change you personally shipped or observed shipping. Sketch its value stream from idea to production, marking every working stage and every wait. Estimate process time and lead time, compute the rough flow efficiency, and circle the single longest queue. Note whether QA feedback happened before or after that queue.$L$, $L$On the map you know best, at which stage are most defects injected, and at which stage are they typically discovered? What does the distance between those two points cost, and what would it take to shorten it?$L$, $L$An SDLC map shows a change with 4 days of process time and 22 days of lead time, most of the gap being a late 5-day manual regression and repeated waits for a stable environment. What does the large gap between process time and lead time tell a QA leader about where to focus? (Answer: the system loses time and quality in queues and late, batched feedback — not in the touch-time activities; the focus should be on compressing feedback loops and the late inspection stage, not on making people work faster)$L$, array[$L$I can map an SDLC as it truly operates, capturing hand-offs, queues and rework$L$, $L$I can overlay QA touchpoints and see whether quality is built in or inspected in$L$, $L$I can use the process-time versus lead-time gap to locate where the system loses time and quality$L$]
